until the monsoon rains came last week  barkat ali's was the newest house in the  village  it started raining my neighbors sent  their children to my house as well  because my place was new it was safe  there was no fear  suddenly at two in the morning there was  a loud crash  and the entire roof came down  sixteen women and children were asleep  inside at the time  ali's wife young son and three other  children were crushed  he feels helpless he says his household  wiped away  traditional structures in rural sindh  affordable for the poor usually suited  to the terrain are being overwhelmed by  the effects of climate change  the weather has become so extreme so  suddenly people say the building  techniques their fathers and  grandfathers use to make houses just  aren't strong enough anymore  pakistan produces less than one percent  of global carbon emissions but suffers  some of its worst effects  muhammad hussain lost two daughters and  a son when the roof collapsed  a message to the world from a grieving  father  my of my friend  the rain was not like this before i just  want to tell the world prepare  yourselves go somewhere safe  my advice is don't sleep under roofs may  god keep you all safe i wouldn't wish  what happened to us on anyone  his wife was in the house at the time  she now clings to the children she has  left  still in shock she hasn't said much  since it happened  they put their children to bed where  they thought it would be dry where they  thought it would be safe  men slept out in the open to give women  and children more room  the best of intentions  met with the worst of fates  zain basravi al-jazeera shakarpur sindh  province pakistan
 

